-Every provision is a default (GOV.19), which a project replaces by a recorded decision with an alternative that meets the same higher-level outcome (REPRO.7). The failures of OpenMP code are governed by provisions that already bind at every class: PRIME.4 for silent continuation past an error, STND.CONC.1 and STND.CONC.2 for asynchronous work and the failures of tasks, STND.CODE.7 for failures that code receives, HW.8 for faults, and HW.GPU.3 for offloaded work; STND.OMP.16 gives the OpenMP means where code relies on cancellation. The standard is compiler, vendor, implementation, and tool agnostic: its provisions name no implementation, compiler, runtime, checker, or revision of the OpenMP specification, which they take at the revision that the work's implementation documents, and they name directives, clauses, and routines of OpenMP by name and not by section (GOV.18). The rules of concurrency that hold for every model are in STND.CONC; the model-neutral rules of accelerator offload belong to a planned standard of their own; and the C++ rules for exceptions at the boundary of a construct are in STND.CXX.
