 The routines that set the number of threads or dynamic adjustment set the internal control variable of the current task, which every later region of the caller inherits, so a library that calls them changes the behavior of its caller (STND.OMP.19, the OpenMP form of STND.CONC.17). A team is able to have fewer threads than requested (STND.OMP.20). A branch or a longjmp that leaves a structured block does not conform, and a cancellation transfers control to the end of the canceled region (unverified in r1 to r4), which STND.OMP.21 admits; STND.CXX.22 governs exceptions. The specification lets a signal handler execute only directives and routines with the property of being safe in a signal handler; in revision 6.0 only the entry points of tools have it, by inference from a search of that property (r2, section 6) (STND.OMP.22). The Examples of the specification warn that the resources of an OpenMP runtime may have unexpected side effects for a later call that creates a child by copying the process, and point to the routine that pauses those resources (r2, section 5); STND.CONC.19 governs.
 
-The rules for offload rest on research/openmp-notes/r3-offload.md. A target region runs on the host when no device is available or supported, or when its if clause is false, and under the default offload policy it does so silently; mandatory offload ends the run instead where no device is available or supported, but a false if clause still runs the region on the host, by inference from r3; the report that the second alternative of STND.OMP.23 asks is not part of the first, and HW.19, HW.9, and the test of the path on the host in its second sentence cover the false if clause (STND.OMP.23). A device number that does not conform ends the run under mandatory offload, and otherwise has behavior that depends on the internal control variables; the default device is itself set by the environment (unverified in r1 to r4), so a number obtained from it is checked like any other; the specification pairs the routine that allocates device memory with the routine that releases it, and that routine returns a null pointer on failure, which STND.CODE.7, PRIME.4, and HW.8 already have code check (r3) (STND.OMP.24). Wrong map and nowait clauses were the main causes of mapping errors in one study (r4): a scalar referenced in a target region is firstprivate, so the writes of the device to it are lost; a pointer whose target is not mapped keeps its host address; a structure mapped in part and referenced in full does not conform; an update of data that is not present on the device does nothing unless it carries the present modifier; and a copy back to the host happens at the release of the last mapping of the data, or under the always modifier or an update construct, and not at the end of a nested mapping that the reference count keeps (STND.OMP.25 and STND.OMP.26). The device copy of a variable that a declare target directive names is initialized once, at an unspecified point, and the two copies diverge without an update; whether a variable of static storage is visible on a device without such a directive is implementation defined (STND.OMP.27). A device that does not meet a requires directive of the program is not a supported device, so code that relies on memory shared between host and device falls back to the host, or fails, on a device with separate memory, and one implementation documents dropping such devices; the specification requires every compilation unit with device code to declare the same requirements; and shared memory still needs synchronization between host and device (STND.OMP.28). The default memory scope of an atomic construct is the device, so an atomic construct without a wider scope does not order accesses between host threads and device threads (STND.OMP.29). The floating-point environment inside and after a target region is unspecified, and the mathematical functions and the contraction of operations on a device differ from those of the host (STND.OMP.30); HW.5 has the hardware contract state the delivered arithmetic of each path, and STND.VVUQ.1 gives the tier of a comparison between them. Most of these rules are specific to the directive model of OpenMP. Completion before results are read, the movement of data, and device arithmetic hold for every offload model, and belong to the planned standard for offload.
